{"causes":["Suffering from illness, Bayeux residents sought spiritual guidance from Saint Marcouf.","The early Christian church's emphasis on prayer and fasting influenced Saint Marcouf's own spiritual practices.","A local epidemic led to the widespread practice of scrofula healing under Saint Marcouf's care."],"effects":["The scrofula healing practices attributed to Saint Marcouf were adopted by other Christian communities, contributing to the spread of Christianity in Europe.","The spiritual guidance offered by Saint Marcouf helped shape the identity and values of the people of Bayeux.","The legacy of Saint Marcouf continues to inspire individuals seeking spiritual growth and healing today.","Saint Marcouf's life and teachings have been referenced in various historical accounts, ensuring his place in the annals of Christian history."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"bayeux","sourceName":"Bayeux","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"as a spiritual leader and healer."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-church","sourceName":"The Early Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"in the adoption of prayer and fasting practices."},{"sourceSlug":"bayeux-epidemic","sourceName":"Bayeux Epidemic","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"the widespread practice of scrofula healing under Saint Marcouf's care."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-church","sourceName":"The Early Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"through the continued reference to Saint Marcouf's life and teachings in historical accounts."},{"sourceSlug":"bible","sourceName":"The Bible","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"in various passages describing the early Christian era and the practices of Saint Marcouf."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-church","sourceName":"The Early Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"the spiritual guidance and leadership offered by Saint Marcouf to other Christian communities."},{"sourceSlug":"baptist-church","sourceName":"The Baptist Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"in discussions of the early Christian era and the practices of Saint Marcouf."},{"sourceSlug":"christian-church","sourceName":"The Early Christian Church","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"saint-marcouf","targetName":"Saint Marcouf","context":"as a significant figure in the early Christian era and a key player in the development of spiritual practices."}],"places":[{"name":"Nantus, Cotentin Peninsula","role":"Abbot's Residence"},{"name":"Bayeux","role":"Place of Healing and Spiritual Guidance"},{"name":"Cotentin Peninsula","role":"Region of Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Saint Marcouf","type":"Reference article","slug":"saint-marcouf"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q685409","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marculf"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Saint Marcouf (also Marculf, Marculphus; c. 490–558 CE), was a 6th-century Frankish abbot and monastic founder in Normandy whose post-mortem cult acquired a remarkable royal association: his relics were used in the coronation ceremonies of French kings for centuries, as the royal 'touching for scrofula' (the king's evil) was traditionally preceded or accompanied by veneration of Saint Marcouf's relics. This association between a Norman monastic saint and the most symbolic expression of French royal theocratic power — the miraculous healing of scrofula by the king's touch, which was presented as evidence of divine mandate — made Marcouf one of the minor but distinctively important figures in the history of medieval French kingship.

Born in Bayeux of a noble family, Marcouf was ordained a priest and initially served in the pastoral ministry of the Cotentin peninsula in northwestern Normandy. He later established a monastery on the island of Nanteuil (near Coutances), creating one of the monastic communities that were multiplying across Frankish Gaul in the 6th century as the Merovingian church built its institutional infrastructure. He had a reputation for asceticism and miraculous healing during his lifetime, which formed the basis of his post-mortem cult. His monastery at Nanteuil (later known as Saint-Marcouf) became a centre of pilgrimage and preserved his relics, which were translated several times during the Norman Invasions and the Carolingian period.

The royal association came through the transfer of his relics to Corbeny (in the Aisne), near Laon, in the 9th century — the site was close enough to Reims, the coronation city of French kings, to become incorporated into the coronation ritual. From the Capetian period onward, newly crowned French kings made a pilgrimage to Corbeny to touch Marcouf's relics before performing the royal touching for scrofula — a ceremony that Marc Bloch analysed in his landmark study 'The Royal Touch' (1924) as evidence for the sacred character attributed to medieval kingship.","subjects":["Hagiography","Medieval France","Church History","Classical Era","Normandy","Medieval Era","Kingship","Merovingian History"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Saint Marcouf's life and teachings had a profound impact on the development of spiritual practices in Christianity, shaping the identity and values of individuals and communities.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[],"_unsyncedEdits":false}
